::ENG3_U06_L01_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which presentation focus is narrow and clear? {
~A. My presentation is about school, books, speeches, and technology.#Incorrect. This focus is too broad. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Credible evidence helps listeners evaluate a speaker's claim.#Correct. This focus is specific and communication-centered. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. I will say many things about a topic.#Incorrect. This is vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. Evidence exists in lots of places.#Incorrect. This is too general. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Draft order: closing, evidence, focus, explanation. Which order is clearest? {
~A. Closing, focus, evidence, explanation.#Incorrect. The closing should not begin the presentation. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Evidence, focus, explanation, closing.#Incorrect. Evidence before focus can confuse listeners. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Explanation, focus, evidence, closing.#Incorrect. Explanation needs the focus and evidence first. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. Focus, evidence, explanation, closing.#Correct. This gives listeners a logical sequence. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source sentence: The article explains that paraphrasing evidence can make complex information easier for listeners to understand. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
=A. The article says putting evidence in your own words can help listeners understand difficult information.#Correct. This preserves the meaning. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The article says listeners should never hear evidence.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The article says paraphrasing makes information more confusing.#Incorrect. This is the opposite. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The article says speakers should copy every source exactly.#Incorrect. The source supports paraphrasing. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Context: Which phrase is the best academic replacement for "facts people can trust"? {
~A. good stuff#Incorrect. This is informal and vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. random details#Incorrect. Random details are not necessarily trustworthy. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. credible evidence#Correct. This is precise academic vocabulary. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. cool information#Incorrect. This is informal. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which evidence best supports a presentation about the value of revision? {
~A. A claim that revision is boring.#Incorrect. This does not support the value of revision. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Data showing revised essays had clearer claims than first drafts.#Correct. This is relevant and credible. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. A personal guess that revision might work.#Incorrect. A guess is weak evidence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. An unrelated statistic about lunch menus.#Incorrect. This does not support the focus. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q06::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The speaker present credible evidence clearly. Which revision is correct? {
=A. The speaker presents credible evidence clearly.#Correct. The singular subject needs "presents." Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The speaker present credible evidence clearly.#Incorrect. Subject and verb do not agree. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The speaker presenting credible evidence clearly.#Incorrect. This is a fragment. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The speaker presentation credible evidence clearly.#Incorrect. "Presentation" is not the correct verb. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q07::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: A listener says, "I heard several examples, but I never understood the main point." What should the speaker improve? {
~A. Add more unrelated examples.#Incorrect. More examples will not fix missing focus. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Remove all evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is still needed. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. Speak faster.#Incorrect. Speed does not fix focus. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=D. State a clear focus before giving evidence.#Correct. The audience needs the main point first. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q08::[MLA.ENG3.R.10] Source sentence: The survey found that students remembered presentations better when speakers organized ideas in clear sections. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Students remembered presentations less when organization was clear.#Incorrect. This reverses the finding. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Speakers should avoid sections during presentations.#Incorrect. The source supports clear sections. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. The survey showed that clear sections helped students remember presentations.#Correct. This keeps the meaning. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. The survey found that organization never matters.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q09::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which opening best prepares listeners for an organized presentation? {
~A. I have a lot of things to say and will start somewhere.#Incorrect. This lacks focus. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=B. Today I will explain two ways credible evidence strengthens oral presentations.#Correct. This previews focus and organization. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~C. This topic is about many different ideas.#Incorrect. This is vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. I did not organize this, but here it goes.#Incorrect. This weakens audience confidence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q10::[MLA.ENG3.V.01] Context: Which sentence uses academic vocabulary appropriately in a presentation? {
=A. The source strengthens my claim because it provides credible evidence.#Correct. "Strengthens," "claim," and "credible evidence" are academic and accurate. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~B. The source makes my stuff sound good.#Incorrect. This is informal. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~C. The source does a thing to my idea.#Incorrect. This is vague. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~D. The source is a very source-like source.#Incorrect. This is circular. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q11::[MLA.ENG3.C.01] Context: Which presentation point is most clearly connected to the focus "credible evidence builds audience trust"? {
~A. My favorite speech was short.#Incorrect. This does not connect to credible evidence. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
~B. Some presentations use slides.#Incorrect. This may be true but is not directly connected. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
=C. Listeners are more likely to trust a claim when the speaker explains where the evidence comes from.#Correct. This point directly supports the focus. Notice how the answer uses the exact wording, evidence, or concept required by the standard.
~D. Public speaking can happen in many rooms.#Incorrect. This is unrelated. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
}

::ENG3_U06_L01_Q12::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Draft: The presentation has a focus, it needs stronger evidence. Which revision is correct? {
~A. The presentation has a focus it needs stronger evidence.#Incorrect. This is a run-on. Recheck the passage, context, or task wording and choose the option that preserves meaning and matches the standard.
